  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\n\nWe propose to improve the upper bound for Erd\u0151s' minimum overlap problem by focusing on the following key aspects:\n\n1. **Resolution independence**: We notice that the current implementation transfers only the parent, but the overlap constant might be improved by considering intermediate resolutions that are not equal to the parent's. We will systematically explore a range of resolutions.\n\n2. **Reduced gradient computation**: The numerical gradient computation in `compute_gradient` is computationally expensive. We should optimize this by:\n   - Using sparsity of the Hessian\n   - Gradient projection on the active set\n   - Exploiting separability by lags in the objective function\n\n3. **Multi-level optimization**: We will develop a framework that:\n   - Starts from high-resolution parents\n   - Gradually reduces resolution for local exploitation\n   - Uses analytical gradient information more effectively\n   - Combines different optimization methods strategically\n\nThe central idea is to create a gradient-assisted optimization that minimizes the maximum overlap by treating it as a block-minimization problem. We extend the parent by using a penalty method that incorporates constraints more tightly.\n\n4. **Constraint satisfaction**: We develop a projection method that preserves the integral constraint (sum h[i] = n/2) and bounds (0\u2264h[i]\u22641) more effectively than simple clipping. We use a two-step projection:\n   - First projection onto the simplex (\u222bh=1 constraint)\n   - Second projection onto the box constraints\n\n5. **Objective evaluation**: We compute the objective not just at current points but also using directional derivatives for early termination.\n\nMathematical specification\n\nWe maintain the same representation: h as a step function with n_points samples on [0,2] with dx=2/n_points.\n\nThe objective to minimize is:\n\nC_5 = max_k \u222b h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nThe gradient with respect to h[i] for a fixed k is:\n\n\u2202(overlap_k)/\u2202h[i] = (dx) * [ (1-h(x_initial)) for i but accounted for both h[i] and the convolved term ]\n\nMore precisely, for a shift k:\n\n\u2202(overlap_k)/\u2202h[i] = dx * ( (1 - (h(x+k]))_i if i corresponds to x+k in domain\n             - h[i] * derivative of penalty on h(x+k))\n             - This gradient is non-trivial because of the circular nature of the convolution.\n\nWe formulate this as an unconstrained problem by penalizing violations:\n\nMinimize ||\u2207f| only if we have first-order information.\n\nConstraints are incorporated via a penalty function.\n\nImplementation procedure\n\n1. Precompute helper functions:\n   - `project_to_simplex`: Projects a vector onto the simplex {h | sum(h)=n/2}\n   - `project_to_box`: Projects onto the unit cube [0,1]\n   - `compute_penalty_term`: Defines a smooth approximation of max_k(overlap)\n\n2. Solve with a quasi-Newton method (L-BFGS-B) on a reduced dimension (number of active shifts)\n\n3. Minimize the maximum of the overlaps by:\n\n   a. Iteratively selecting the most constraining shift\n   b. Optimizing the function under that shifted constraint\n\n4. Use Nelder-Mead as a fallback\n\n5. For multi-resolution:\n\n   - Only optimize within fixed higher resolutions\n   - Start from parent and iteratively find lower-resolution parents\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\n\nWe will:\n\n- Sweep the maximum penalty parameter \u03b1 to find a good trade-off\n- Use analytical gradients where possible\n- Implement a warm-start strategy across resolutions\n- Use line search for step size determination\n- Employ sequential optimization over one shift at a time\n\nBudget and robustness\n\nThe plan must complete in <budget_s seconds. We:\n\n- Allocate time budget: 50% for high-resolution, 25% for gradient computation\n- Use caching for objective evaluations\n- Revert to a fallback simplex method if gradient-based method fails\n\nValidation and return contract\n\n1. For each candidate:\n   - Re-evaluate the objective using high-precision integration\n   - Verify 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n   - Check sum(h)\u2248n/2 (numerically)\n   - Maintain a global best candidate\n\n2. Return the best candidate found with:\n   - Its h_values\n   - The computed C_5 bound\n   - The number of points\n\nThis plan ensures that we improve upon the parent by systematically addressing the limitations while maintaining numerical stability and efficiency.",
